<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/advanced/writing-systems-of-the-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Writing is speech's interface technology, and scripts are its encoding standards. There are only a handful
    of fundamental designs — and each design is implicitly a <strong>theory of what language is made of</strong>:
    alphabets assume it is phonemes, syllabaries assume syllables, logographies assume morphemes and meanings.
    Writing was invented independently only a few times (Mesopotamia c. 3200 BCE, China c. 1200 BCE,
    Mesoamerica by the first millennium BCE, with Egypt a probable fourth), and every script on earth descends
    from, or reinvents, one of those solutions. This lesson maps the designs, the flagship scripts, and what
    reading science says about how each one trains a different brain.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four system types and their flagship scripts" widget="equationStep" config={scriptCfg} />

  <h2>The four designs and their trade-offs</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Alphabets</strong> segment speech into its smallest units — phonemes — and assign a symbol to
    each: some 20–40 characters cover any word the language can produce. The cost is that phonemes are
    abstractions (nothing in the sound stream isolates "b"), so the system takes explicit teaching; the
    benefit is that a few dozen symbols suffice forever. <strong>Abjads</strong> (Arabic, Hebrew) encode
    consonants and leave short vowels mostly unwritten — economical for Semitic languages, where consonantal
    roots carry core meaning (k-t-b gives kitāb "book," kātib "writer"), and workable once readers know the
    language. <strong>Syllabaries</strong> (Japanese kana, Linear B) assign one symbol per syllable or mora:
    simple to learn (Japanese has about 100 kana), at the cost of one symbol per syllable in the language.
    <strong>Logographies</strong> (Chinese hanzi) assign a character to a morpheme: thousands of symbols, but
    the system is language-independent of pronunciation — a Shanghainese and a Mandarin speaker read the same
    text aloud differently, and Chinese characters remained legible across three millennia of sound change
    precisely because they do not spell sounds. One bonus family: <strong>featural</strong> scripts, whose
    letterforms encode articulation itself — Hangul, the topic below.
  </p>

  <h2>How the scripts travelled: the syllabus's five</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Latin</strong> descends through Etruscan from Greek, which made the Phoenician abjad's crucial
    upgrade: Greek repurposed consonant signs for unused consonants as <strong>vowels</strong> — the first
    full alphabet, and the reason the alphabet idea conquered the world. <strong>Cyrillic</strong> (late 9th
    century) was built by disciples of Cyril and Methodius for Slavic languages, shaped by Greek and
    engineered for Slavic sounds (ж, ш, я) — a reminder that scripts are designed and redesigned. Modern
    Cyrillic serves more than 50 languages. <strong>Devanagari</strong> is the classic <strong>abugida</strong>:
    the consonant carries the base symbol and vowels attach as marks (क + ि = कि), with an inherent "a"
    silenced by a virama — a system phonologically elegant and syllable-shaped, used for Hindi, Marathi,
    Nepali, Sanskrit and dozens more. <strong>Kana</strong> are the moraic syllabaries: hiragana for grammar
    (はをが), katakana for foreign words and emphasis — learned in a weekend with mnemonics, and the
    learner's first unambiguous win in Japanese. <strong>Han</strong> characters are the logographic core:
    roughly 50,000 exist, about 3,000 cover ordinary reading, and educated readers command 4,000–8,000; each
    character often combines a <strong>semantic radical</strong> (the water 氵 in 河 "river") with a
    <strong>phonetic component</strong> (可 kě) — a partially sound-based system hiding inside a logography,
    which is why character learning is pattern-learning, not pure memorisation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most sophisticated scripts on earth are <strong>mixed</strong>. A Japanese sentence routinely uses
    kanji for content words, hiragana for inflections, katakana for loanwords and the Latin alphabet for
    acronyms — four systems interleaved, parsed without pause. Korean shows the reverse migration: Hanja
    largely replaced by <strong>Hangul</strong> (1443), King Sejong's engineered alphabet — consonant
    letters drawn from the mouth's articulators (ㅁ from closed lips, ㅅ the tooth ridge), letters packed
    into syllable blocks, promulgated in the Hunminjeongeum ("correct sounds for the instruction of the
    people") to raise commoner literacy. It is the only script whose design rationale was published with it,
    and the plan worked: Korean literacy is effectively universal. Note the sociolinguistic depth: Vietnam
    and Turkey deliberately <strong>re-scripted</strong> onto Latin (chu Quốc ngữ, 1928 reform in Turkey) —
    script choice is language policy, cutting past literature off from new readers.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> alphabets are "advanced" and logographies "primitive" — a ladder-of-progress
  myth with no linguistic basis. Each design optimises different variables (symbol economy, learnability,
  cross-dialect legibility, cross-time legibility), and Chinese readers read as fast and accurately as
  alphabetic readers. A script is a technology fitted to a language and a society, not a rung on a ladder.</p>

  <h2>Scripts and minds: what reading science finds</h2>
  <p>
    Reading is not natural — it is a cultural invention the brain recycles circuits for, and the
    <strong>script shapes the circuitry</strong>. The <strong>orthographic depth</strong> hypothesis splits
    scripts by spelling-to-sound transparency: Finnish or Spanish are shallow (letters predict sounds),
    English is notoriously deep (though, through), Chinese deepest of all (character to meaning, with
    phonetics unreliable). Consequences show up measurably: children learn to read transparent
    orthographies faster and more completely; dyslexia manifests differently — English dyslexia is
    phonological assembly failure, Chinese reading disability involves more visual-orthographic processing —
    and the two draw on partly different neural networks (Chinese reading engages more bilateral and
    right-hemisphere visual areas; alphabetic reading leans harder on the left temporoparietal phonological
    route). Eye-movement research adds the fine grain: readers of Chinese make shorter saccades over denser
    information; readers of English skip short function words. Yet the deepest finding is an invariance:
    <strong>information throughput in reading is roughly constant across scripts</strong> (Pelli's estimate:
    about 40–50 bits per second regardless of writing system) — alphabets trade more symbols for shorter
    words; logographies trade fewer, denser units; the brain converges on the same bandwidth. No design is
    superior; they are different ways to solve the same rate limit.
  </p>

  <h2>The deep history: how writing invented itself</h2>
  <p>
    Writing's evolution follows a logic worth knowing, because every learner's script recapitulates it.
    In Mesopotamia, accounting <strong>tokens</strong> (Denise Schmandt-Besserat's analysis: clay
    counters for sheep, grain, jars, pressed into envelopes) stiffened into <strong>pictographs</strong> —
    the token for sheep drawn on the tablet. The decisive trick was the <strong>rebus
    principle</strong>: write the sound, not the thing — the drawing of a reed (Sumerian <em>gi</em>)
    reused to spell the syllable <em>gi</em> in any word — which unlocks syllables from pictures and
    makes full language writable. Cuneiform then stylised the pictures under the stylus; Chinese oracle
    bone script followed the same pictograph-to-character path; Mayan glyphs recorded full language by
    the first millennium BCE. The <strong>alphabet</strong> was the final compression and arguably the
    strangest: Semitic scribes (Egyptian-influenced) noticed they needed only the consonants; Greek
    borrowers, whose language could not tolerate that, repurposed leftover signs as vowels — the one
    genuine conceptual leap in the system's history, made once, inherited by Latin, Cyrillic, Armenian
    and dozens more. Every script lesson you will ever take — kana in a weekend, Arabic shapes, Hangul
    blocks — is a fast-forward through these same pressures: economy of symbols against explicitness,
    with each civilisation cutting the trade-off differently.
  </p>

  <h2>Scripts as identity: digraphia and script choice</h2>
  <p>
    Because scripts are learned, public and political, they carry identity the way flags do — and
    <strong>digraphia</strong> (one language, two scripts) is the visible seam. Hindi and Urdu are, at
    the spoken colloquial level, substantially one language (Hindustani) — written in Devanagari and
    Arabic-based Nastaliq respectively, with the two literatures pulling the registers apart. Serbian
    officially runs Cyrillic and Latin in parallel. Norwegian standardises two written varieties
    (Bokmål and Nynorsk) from different dialect bases. The design lesson from these cases: script choice
    says who the reader community is — states have re-scripted populations deliberately (Turkey 1928,
    the Central Asian Latinisations, Soviet Cyrillicisations), and script revival (Hebrew print's
    modernisation, Cherokee's syllabary, N'Ko for Mande languages) is nation- and community-building by
    typographic means. When you learn a script, you are also joining a historically positioned community
    of readers — which is why script-learning feels like more than a technical milestone, and is.
  </p>

  <h2>Learning a script: the pedagogy, by design</h2>
  <p>
    Because scripts differ structurally, their learning curves differ predictably, and the study plan
    should follow the structure. <strong>Kana</strong> (46 hiragana + 46 katakana, plus voicing marks):
    mnemonics and a weekend of writing practice — the win comes fast because the mapping is small and
    closed. <strong>Arabic</strong>: learn the letter shapes <em>by position</em> (isolated, initial,
    medial, final — the cursive joining is the system, not decoration), and practise short-vowel-less
    reading with known vocabulary until the root-recognition instinct forms. <strong>Devanagari</strong>:
    master the consonant + inherent-a logic first, then the vowel signs and conjuncts — reading
    practise with the horizontal headline (shirorekha) does much of the segmentation work for you.
    <strong>Hangul</strong>: a week of the letter shapes and block-building rules gives real literacy
    in pronunciation, the designed script's promise kept. <strong>Han</strong>: the only long game —
    sequence by frequency (the top 1,000 characters cover the vast majority of running text), learn
    radicals as the filing system, and accept that reading fluency arrives by volume, not by
    flashcarding to completion. Two habits pay off across all of them: write by hand (motor memory
    anchors recognition), and move to real text the moment you can stumble through it — scripts, like
    languages, are learned by use.
  </p>

  <h2>The syllabus scripts, side by side</h2>
  <p>
    Consolidate the five with one comparison table in prose. <strong>Latin</strong>: ~26 letters, phonemic
    mapping of moderate depth (English the famous outlier), written left to right — the world's most used
    script by country count. <strong>Cyrillic</strong>: Greek's heir adapted for Slavic sounds, ~33 letters
    in Russian, transparent in most of its languages. <strong>Devanagari</strong>: abugida — each consonant
    inherently carries "a," vowel signs and the virama modify, conjuncts stack; phonologically exact once
    learned. <strong>Kana</strong>: two moraic syllabaries in parallel — hiragana for native grammar,
    katakana for borrowed words, the only script family with a built-in register distinction.
    <strong>Han</strong>: morpheme-syllable characters, partially phonetic via radicals, readable across
    dialects and centuries, costly in character count. Each answers one design brief: how much of the
    sound to encode, at what learning price, for which language. Once you can articulate each brief — not
    just name each script — you can analyse any writing system you meet, including the ones not yet
    invented.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Writing changed cognition — and that claim has a history.</strong> Jack Goody's "literacy thesis"
    argued writing enabled logic, lists and history; anthropologists and psychologists dismantled the strong
    version (literate societies were not suddenly rational), but the refined claim survives: literacy
    rewires <em>individual</em> brains measurably (the "visual word form area" develops only through reading;
    literate brains differ from illiterate ones in phonemic awareness — letters teach phonemes as much as
    phonemes teach letters — a genuine reverse causation discovered by reading science). <strong>Digital
    scripts</strong> are the current frontier: Unicode now encodes over 150 scripts (including historically
    significant revivals like Cherokee syllabary and N'Ko), and the engineering questions are live — fonts
    for complex shaping (Devanagari conjuncts, Arabic ligatures), bidirectional text, emoji's own quasi-
    logography. <strong>Multilingual cognition</strong> closes the loop with the psycholinguistics lesson:
    readers of two scripts run two visual systems in parallel (a Thai–English bilingual makes different
    eye movements per script), orthographic neighbours from each language compete during recognition, and
    script acquisition adds a genuinely separate learning problem on top of language acquisition. Open
    questions: will predictive text and voice input shift scripts' evolution from community to corporation;
    does character-based reading, with its holistic demands, produce measurably different attention; and
    can under-resourced scripts get the computational infrastructure (shaping engines, fonts, corpora)
    their literatures deserve — a question where linguistics, engineering and language policy meet.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Coulmas, <em>The Writing Systems of the World</em>; Sampson, <em>Writing Systems</em>; King Sejong's Hunminjeongeum (1446); Katz &amp; Frost on orthographic depth; Pelli &amp; Tillman (2007); Dehaene, <em>Reading in the Brain</em> (2009).</p>
